entry.summary:
The back of an archival glass-plate transparency shows the State of Liberty in reverse (Lady Liberty actually holds her torch in her right hand). Getting this shot in 1930 required a feat of technical imagination.
Few had glimpsed the world in color from above, until a maverick <i>National Geographic</i> editor hatched a plan to capture the Statue of Liberty in full glory.&nbsp;
Some of the earliest aerial photos ever taken in color were committed to gelatin-coated glass plates now held in the National Geographic archives. They include this sweeping shot of the Statue of Liberty, shown here on the back of an old plate rimmed with fraying labels. The scene was captured in 1930 by National Geographic magazine Assistant Editor (and future Editor in Chief) Melville Bell Grosvenor—a photo it wo…
